tion for k=1 for general n.\n\nWe have n lines total. Suppose we use n-1 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-1. That covers all points except those in column n (only point (n,1)). Then we need a sunny line to cover (n,1). That's fine. So we have n-1 vertical lines (non-sunny) and one sunny line covering (n,1). That's exactly k=1. So that works for any n≥3. Indeed, vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1 cover all points except (n,1). Then we add a sunny line through (n,1) with any slope not 0,∞,-1. So k=1 works. So the solution's claim that k=1 works is correct.\n\nNow k=3: we need exactly three sunny lines. We can try to use n-3 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3. That covers all points in those columns. Then we need to cover points in columns n-2, n-1, n. Column n-2 has points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). Column n-1 has (n-1,1),(n-1,2). Column n has (n,1). That's total 6 points. We have 3 sunny lines left to cover these 6 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column (since any line covering two points in same column is vertical). So each sunny line can cover at most 3 points (one from each column). So with 3 sunny lines, we can cover at most 9 points, which is enough for 6 points. But we need to ensure that we can choose 3 sunny lines such that each covers one point from each column, and all points are covered. Since we have 6 points, we need to assign each point to a line. Each line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover multiple points across columns. So we need to find 3 lines that collectively cover all 6 points, with each line covering at most one point from each column. This is like a matching problem: we need to partition the 6 points into 3 groups, each group containing at most one point from each column, and each group must be collinear (i.e., points in a group must lie on a line). Since we have only 2 points in column n-1 and 1 point in column n, we can try to pair them with points from column n-2.\n\nWe need to find 3 lines L1, L2, L3